L254: 7.2 QUANTUM-ELECTRODYNAMICS
L255: Quantum relativistic perturbation-theory of electromagnetic interactions.
L256: Most papers relate to the attempt of R.P. Feynman's student L.M. Brown to reformulate quantum electrodynamics using, instead of the usual four-component-spinor Dirac matrix formalism, the two-component-spinor Pauli-matrix formalism which is essentially equivalent to that of biquaternions due to the isomorphism \mathbb{B} \sim M_2(\mathbb{C}).
L257: 1. R.P. Feynman, An operator calculus having applications in quantum electrodynamics, Phys. Rev. 84 (1951) 108–128. 2. L.M. Brown, Two-component fermion theory, Phys. Rev. 109 (1958) 193–198. 3. T.W.B. Kibble and J.C. Polkinghorne, Higher order spinor lagrangians, Nuovo Cim. 8 (1958) 74–83. 4. M. Tonin, Quantization of the two-component fermion theory, Nuovo Cim. 14 (1959) 1108–1119. 5. H. Pietschmann, Zur Renormierung der zweikomponentigen Quantenelektrodynamik, Acta Physica Austriaca 14 (1961) 63–74. 6. A.O. Barut and G.H. Mullen, Quantization of two-component higher order spinor equations, Ann. Phys. 20 (New York, 1962) 184–202. 7. L.M. Brown, Two-component fermion theory, Lectures in Theoretical Physics 4 (Interscience, New York, 1962) 324–357. 8. P. Rotelli, Quaternion trace theorems and first order electron-muon scattering, Mod. Phys. Lett. A 4 (1989) 1763–1771. 9. H.T. Cho, A. Diek, and R. Kantowski, A Clifford algebra quantization of Dirac's electron-positron field, J. Math. Phys. 31 (1990) 2192–2200.
