L314: 10 Conventions used in the bibliography
L315: 10.1 Conventions for the \TeX citation-label
L316: The \TeX citation-label has always 10 characters, i.e.:
L317: LASTN1234- or LASTN1234X ,
L318: were LASTN are the 5 first characters of the last name, “1234” the four-digit year, and “X” is A, B, C, ..., Z if there are more than one reference for the author in that year.
L319: If the author last name has less than 5 characters, the missing characters are replaced by “-”s.
L320: N.B.: In the case where different authors have the same last name the 10th character (i.e., “-”) will be used to remove possible ambiguities that may arise in a given year.
L321: 10.2 Conventions for the references's "style"
L322: Authors: Only first character of first-names. Ex: P.A.M. Dirac.
L323: Composite last-names are concatenated: De Broglie → DeBroglie.
L324: Two authors: Use “and” between their names.
L325: Three or more authors: Use “, and” before the last author in the list.
L326: Book titles: Capitalize First Letter of Each Word. Normal characters.
L327: Article titles: Capitalize only first letter of first word. Italic characters.
L328: Editor(s): Use “ed.” or “eds.”
L329: Latin: Use only unambiguous combinations such as: “et al.”, “ibid.”, “in:”...
L330: Volume numbers: Bold characters.
L331: No other bold symbol should appear anywhere in the bibliography file.
L332: Number of pages in a book: “123 pp.” (Non-break space: “\sim” in \TeX.)
L333: First and last pages of article: “123–456” (Medium dash: “--” in \TeX.)
L334: Examples of typical references are given in Sec. 10.6.
L335: 10.3 Internet URLs and arXiv.org links
L336: In order that they are properly processed by the arXiv.org \TeX-compiler, a white space “_” is put in the front and in the back of all internet URLs and arXiv.org links. Moreover, they are underlined. These conventions insure that the URLs and links are properly recognized, and that they are not hyphenated.
L337: For instance, clicking the arXiv.org link arXiv:name/number automatically generates the internet URL http://www.arXiv.org/abs/name/number.
L338: 10.4 Non-\TeX conventions: directives and keywords
L339: In the file special \TeX comments are used to introduce the keywords, and to introduce directives to the bibliography update/search/display computer programs:
L340: • Standard comments start with “%”• Bibliography KEYWORDS start with “%%”• Bibliography directives start with “%$”
L341: 10.5 Directives
L342: • %$D : date of entry in DDMMYYYY format• %$C : a commentary/appreciation of the document follows• %$L : this reference is on loan to the person whose name follows• %$M : this reference is missing or lost• %$N : this reference is not yet filed in its envelope/folder• %$O : this reference is on order from some library• etc.
L343: The date of entry is particularly important for making automatic updates. It is normally the last item in a bibliography entry, unless there is a commentary which is appended at the very end of it.
L344: 10.6 Format of typical references
L345: What follows is a list of typical references in which the label, keywords, and directives are made visible.
